<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Trastámara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1369">1369</a>–<a href="/years/1555">1555</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Trastámara was a Spanish royal dynasty named after the county of Trastámara in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>. It came to the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in 1369, when Henry II killed his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-of-castile">Peter the Cruel</a>, and to the Crown of Aragon in 1412 through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/compromise-of-caspe">Compromise of Caspe</a>. The marriage of Isabella I and Ferdinand II joined the two crowns.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-trastamara.jpg" alt="House of Trastámara" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heralder · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_Arms_of_Queen_Isabella_of_Castile_(1492-1504).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Trastámaras were an illegitimate cadet line of the ruling House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. Their name comes from the County of Trastámara, one of the titles held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-castile">Henry II of Castile</a>, the founder of the dynasty, before he became king. When Alfonso XI died in 1350, his illegitimate children by Eleanor de Guzmán, known collectively as the Trastámaras, became rivals of his legitimate son Peter I. Henry of Trastámara, with the support of Aragon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> led by Bertram Du Guesclin, defeated Peter in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castilian-civil-war">Castilian Civil War</a> of 1351 to 1369, in which the nobility played a decisive part, and killed him himself in 1369.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under Henry II a new nobility rose, gaining large estates and royal privileges, and its rise caused discontent and instability. His son John I succeeded in 1379 and died unexpectedly in 1390. Henry III came to the throne at twelve and took control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a>. The change of dynasty also brought a radicalisation of anti-Jewish feeling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, which reached its climax in the pogroms of 1391. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-of-aragon">Martin of Aragon</a> without a legitimate heir, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/compromise-of-caspe">Compromise of Caspe</a> of 1412 installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-aragon">Ferdinand of Antequera</a> as king of Aragon.</p><p>In Castile John II, who came to power in 1418, lacked authority, and nobles fought for influence. His son Henry IV was an unpopular ruler. In 1468 the Treaty of the Bulls of Guisando named his half-sister Isabella as heir. Her marriage in 1469 to Ferdinand, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and heir to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>, broke the pact in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s eyes, and a civil war of about a decade followed over the claims of Isabella and of Henry's daughter Joanna.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Isabella's factions won with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>'s help, and the marriage of Isabella and Ferdinand, both Trastámaras, united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and Aragon in a dynastic union. The house ruled Castile from 1369 to 1504, Aragon from 1412 to 1516, and also <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> for parts of that time. It was succeeded by the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> became king of Castile and Aragon in 1516, even though his mother Joanna lived on, imprisoned from 1509, until 1555.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Trastámara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
